# Financial Analysis
## Overview and Continuity from Phase 1
This section evaluates the Economic dimension of the TELOS feasibility framework for
ReSource. It builds directly on the Expected Benefits and Assumptions stated in the Phase 1
report [8], where the team identified the primary financial benefit as “reduced spending by
reusing surplus assets and lowering storage costs,” and listed as a working assumption that
“the university would be willing to adopt a centralized platform for internal asset exchange if
one were available.” The analysis below turns those qualitative statements into quantified,
formula-backed estimates.
Consistent with the Phase 1 Project Constraints (academic prototype scope, one Fall 2026
semester, a four-student team, and reliance on existing libraries, pretrained models, or APIs
rather than custom-trained models), all figures in this section describe a single-semester pilot
deployment, not a full university-wide rollout. Every number that is not drawn from a cited
public source is explicitly labeled as an assumption, together with the reasoning behind it, so
the figure can be checked and adjusted rather than taken on faith.
Consistent with standard engineering economic-analysis practice, this section treats
engineering labor as the primary, market-priced cost driver of the project rather than as a free
resource. Personnel time is typically the dominant cost in a software initiative, and pricing it at
a real market rate gives a realistic picture of what building and operating ReSource actually
costs. The current development team's labor carries no cash outlay at this stage, which is
noted separately for transparency, but that is not the basis for the economic case below.
Two categories of cost are analyzed: nonrecurring (one-time) costs and recurring (ongoing
operational) costs, followed by a benefit analysis and a combined cost-benefit summary.
