# Legal and regulatory analysis
## Overview and Scope
This section covers the Legal dimension of the TELOS framework in full: legal, ethical, privacy,
security, procurement, ownership, and safety constraints, together with the university-policy
context ReSource would operate under, not only the intellectual-property and data-privacy
angle shown on the Phase 2 slide deck. It is grounded in three UAE federal laws and two real,
currently published Khalifa University web pages, cited below, and it builds directly on the
Phase 1 report’s [8] stakeholder roles, AI Matching Engine description, and Project
Constraints.
As with the Financial (Economic) Feasibility section above, every legal point is tied to a cited
primary or secondary source; where no public Khalifa University policy document could be
found to cite, that gap is either filled using a simulated stakeholder interview (documented in
Appendix A) or stated openly as an unresolved open item under University Policy
Dependencies and Open Items below rather than filled with an invented citation.
## Data Privacy and Protection: UAE PDPL (Federal Decree-Law No. 45 of 2021)
ReSource stores personal data belonging to Khalifa University students and staff, including
names, university IDs, department affiliations, and asset request/transfer history, which brings
the system within scope of the UAE’s Federal Decree-Law No. 45 of 2021 on the Protection
of Personal Data (PDPL) [12], [13], the UAE’s general federal data-protection law.
• Consent (Article 6): the data controller must be able to prove the data subject’s
consent where consent is the lawful basis for processing, and users may withdraw
consent at any time. ReSource’s account-registration flow needs an explicit, clear
consent step before storing user or asset data.
• Data subject rights: the PDPL grants individuals the right to access their data,
request correction or erasure, restrict processing, and, most relevant to the AI
Matching Engine, the right to object to automated decision-making that has a legal or
similarly significant effect on them.
• Right to object to automated decisions: because the AI Matching Engine assigns a
compatibility score and recommendation for each request, a user who disputes or is
negatively affected by a match should be able to request a human review, not just an
automated explanation.
Data Protection Impact Assessment (Article 21): a DPIA is required before
processing that involves systematic, large-scale automated evaluation with legal or
significant effect. Because the AI Matching Engine performs exactly this kind of
systematic automated evaluation, the team should treat producing a DPIA as a
Phase 3/4 deliverable-adjacent task, not an afterthought.
• Penalties: the PDPL itself does not fix specific fine amounts; it allows the UAE
Cabinet to set administrative penalties through Executive Regulations. This is noted
here as a compliance risk to plan for, rather than a number to quantify, since no
public Executive Regulation fine schedule could be cited.
## Intellectual Property and AI-Output Ownership: UAE Copyright Law (Federal Decree-Law No. 38 of 2021)
ReSource generates AI-authored content: the Matching Engine’s compatibility explanations
and the “Generative AI Reporting” (financial savings, waste diversion, and avoided-purchase
summaries) noted in the Phase 1 stakeholder table for University Management. UAE Federal
Decree-Law No. 38 of 2021 on Copyright and Neighboring Rights [14] protects works that
show originality and human creativity, and, consistent with the position most jurisdictions
currently take [15], does not recognize an AI system itself as an “author.”
• Practical effect: content generated by the AI Matching Engine or the reporting feature
with little human involvement sits in a legal gray area, since a system cannot be
classified as the natural-person author copyright protection normally requires.
• Recommended mitigation: document that KU (as the commissioning institution) owns
system-generated reports and matching explanations through the system’s terms of
use, and log sufficient human review/curation of AI outputs (e.g., a staff member
approving a generated sustainability report before it is shared) to support that
ownership claim, the approach current legal commentary [15] recommends where
the law itself is silent.
• Codebase ownership: who owns the ReSource source code itself at this development
stage is a separate, open question the Phase 1 report does not address and that is
outside the scope of this feasibility analysis; it is listed as an open item under
University Policy Dependencies and Open Items below for Khalifa University's
relevant office to confirm.
## Cybersecurity and Unauthorized Access: UAE Cybercrime Law (Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2021)
ReSource is, functionally, a database of university user and asset records, which places it
squarely within the scope of Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2021 on Countering Rumors and
Cybercrimes [16]. The law criminalizes unauthorized access to IT systems and sensitive data,
with penalties of 6 months to 5 years’ imprisonment and fines ranging from AED 20,000 to
AED 3,000,000 depending on the severity of the offense [17]; it also requires clear consent
before personal data is collected, transmitted, or processed.
• Implication for design: role-based access control should follow the stakeholder roles
already defined in the Phase 1 stakeholder table (e.g., the Procurement Team can
view inventory; University Management has final budget/adoption authority), so that
access is scoped to what each role legitimately needs.
• Recommended controls: authentication, audit logging of who viewed or changed
asset and transfer records, and encryption of data in transit and at rest, all standard
measures that directly reduce exposure under this law’s unauthorized-access
provisions.
• KU single sign-on (SSO): per a simulated interview with an IT Department
Representative (AI Stakeholder Mimic; see Appendix A, Q2), SSO integration is
possible in principle for an approved internal tool but requires a formal KU IT request
and review, not something a course prototype should assume by default. The design
should treat standalone authentication as the working assumption for the prototype,
and SSO integration as a Phase 3/4 dependency to raise with KU IT.
## Procurement, Institutional Ownership, and Sustainability Alignment (Khalifa University Policy)
Two real, currently published Khalifa University web pages give the project direct institutional
grounding, beyond the three federal laws above.
• KU’s Procurement & Contracts Department [18] states that “procurement is more
than just buying things; it’s about making thoughtful choices that help our planet and
future generations,” and describes sustainability-linked supplier engagement and a
formal Procurement Policy document. ReSource operationalizes exactly this principle
(reduce new purchases by matching internal surplus first) at the department level,
which is a strong feasibility argument, but it also means the tool intersects with real
budget-authorization and procurement rules. The team should formally involve this
department during Phase 3/4 review, since any system that influences whether a
department buys new or reuses existing assets touches procurement policy and
University Management’s budget authority, as reflected in the Phase 1 stakeholder
table. This is consistent with a simulated University Management Representative (AI
Stakeholder Mimic; Appendix A, Q3), who noted that final buy-new-vs-reuse
decisions still go through the department’s budget holder and, above a routine
amount, through Procurement, meaning ReSource should surface and support the
reuse case, not make the final spending decision itself.
• KU’s Sustainability Initiative [19] already runs a “Recycle, Reduce & Reuse” program,
reports a 3% reduction in energy consumption from 2022, and aligns its work with the
UAE Water Security Strategy 2036 and UAE Energy Strategy 2050. ReSource
extends these existing, university-endorsed commitments into an AI-assisted
exchange tool, which gives the project institutional legitimacy and a natural internal
sponsor, the KU Sustainability Office, in addition to the IT department.
## Ethical and Safety Considerations
Matching fairness: the AI Matching Engine’s ranking should not systematically
disadvantage smaller departments with fewer registered assets; matching criteria
and the “clear explanation” the Phase 1 report requires for each compatibility score
should be documented and reviewable, so bias can actually be checked rather than
assumed away.Physical safety in the asset life cycle: the Phase 1 report’s
repair/refurbish/donate/recycle/retire life cycle covers general surplus items, but
some assets (lab chemicals, batteries, equipment with electrical or sharp-edge
hazards) need safe-handling and proper e-waste disposal, not just a software match.
Hazardous-material items should be flagged for manual safety review rather than
auto-matched, to avoid a safety liability gap the current workflow does not explicitly
close.
• Data minimization: only asset and request data actually needed for matching should
be collected, in line with the PDPL principles discussed above under Data Privacy
and Protection, rather than defaulting to collecting everything a form could capture.
