# Operational Feasibility
## Overview and Scope
Overview and Scope
This section evaluates the Operational dimension of the TELOS feasibility framework for
ReSource: whether the departments, asset custodians, administrators, maintenance staff,
procurement staff, and sustainability officers identified in the Phase 1 stakeholder table can
realistically adopt the workflows ReSource proposes. Consistent with the Financial
(Economic) Feasibility and Legal, Regulatory and Ethical Feasibility sections above, this
assessment is scoped to a single Fall 2026 semester pilot with roughly 150 active users, not
a full university-wide rollout, and every judgment below is stated as an assumption or a direct
consequence of the stakeholder roles the team has already defined, rather than as confirmed
organizational fact.
## Current-State to Future-State Workflow Change
The Background and Context section above describes the current process as informal and
undocumented: surplus assets are stored, reported for disposal, or mentioned to other
departments by email or word of mouth; requests are raised without checking existing surplus;
transfer approvals happen through email or paper forms with no standard workflow; and
maintenance records live in separate department spreadsheets or paper logs. Operational
feasibility depends on how large a jump ReSource represents from that baseline for each
stakeholder group, since the size of the change, not just its direction, determines whether it is
adopted.
For most of the identified stakeholders, ReSource formalizes and centralizes a task they
already perform informally rather than asking them to take on an entirely new responsibility:
department representatives already decide what their department needs and what it can
spare; administrators already approve transfers and disposals in some form; asset
coordinators already track condition and location, even if only on paper. This matters
operationally, because a recent large-scale meta-analysis of technology-acceptance studies
finds that perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use remain the strongest predictors of
whether a system is actually adopted, with no significant weakening of that effect despite
decades of technological change [20], in practice, that means a system is adopted when target
users judge it as both useful for their existing task and no harder to use than their current
approach. Where ReSource instead asks a stakeholder to take on a genuinely new task, such
as University Management reviewing AI-generated sustainability reports it did not previously
receive, that is flagged separately below as a higher-effort adoption point.
